<!--
  Reference snippet — merge these keys into ios/App/App/Info.plist.
  They register the app as a handler for .gpx/.kml files — which is what makes another
  app able to open a route in MeteoRide — declare the meteoride:// scheme, explain the
  location prompt, and let the ride watch run in the background.
-->

<!-- The two names iOS shows, both MeteoRide, neither of them the project's file name.
     `CFBundleDisplayName` is the one under the icon; `CFBundleName` is the short one iOS
     falls back to where the long one will not fit, and Capacitor's template leaves it as
     $(PRODUCT_NAME), which resolves to "App". The Xcode project must stay App.xcodeproj —
     Capacitor hardcodes that path and stops writing Package.swift otherwise, see
     docs/IOS.md — so this is where the app gets its name instead. Keep both in step. -->
<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
<string>MeteoRide</string>
<key>CFBundleName</key>
<string>MeteoRide</string>

<!-- The two languages the app speaks. Without this the bundle declares English alone, and
     WKWebView tends to hand the page the app's localisations rather than the phone's, so
     navigator.languages — which picks the language at first launch — could say English
     on a Spanish iPhone. NOT yet verified on a device; until it is, the App Review notes
     must not promise the app follows the phone's language. -->
<key>CFBundleLocalizations</key>
<array>
	<string>en</string>
	<string>es</string>
</array>

<!-- Android uses this scheme; on iOS nothing opens it any more, now that the share
     extension is gone. Kept so both platforms declare the same thing, and because
     SceneDelegate ignores non-file URLs by design. -->
<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
<array>
	<dict>
		<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
		<string>cc.meteoride.app</string>
		<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
		<string>Editor</string>
		<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
		<array>
			<string>meteoride</string>
		</array>
	</dict>
</array>

<!-- GPX has no system UTI, so declare one and hang the .gpx extension off it.

     EXPORTED, because the type is ours. Apple: an exported declaration "means that the
     type is available for use by all other parties"; an imported one is for "a type that
     the bundle does not own, but would like to see available on the system". Nobody else
     is ever going to export `cc.meteoride.gpx`, so exported is the honest declaration,
     and where both exist the exported one takes precedence.

     This was CHANGED from imported while chasing a bug, and it is worth writing down that
     it was probably not the bug. Apple says an imported declaration exists precisely so
     the system knows a type "even if the actual [owning] application is not available",
     so the .gpx binding was most likely working all along. What was actually verified on
     a device is narrower: removing the share extension made sharing from Hammerhead open
     MeteoRide. The two changes were made together and never separated, so this one is a
     correctness decision, not a demonstrated cause. -->
<key>UTExportedTypeDeclarations</key>
<array>
	<dict>
		<key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
		<string>cc.meteoride.gpx</string>
		<key>UTTypeDescription</key>
		<string>GPS Exchange Format</string>
		<key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
		<array>
			<string>public.xml</string>
		</array>
		<key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
		<dict>
			<key>public.filename-extension</key>
			<array>
				<string>gpx</string>
			</array>
			<key>public.mime-type</key>
			<array>
				<string>application/gpx+xml</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</dict>
</array>

<!-- KML is Google's type, not ours, so it is IMPORTED — the mirror image of the rule
     above. It still has to be declared by somebody: iOS ships no built-in KML
     identifier, and `CFBundleDocumentTypes` below names `com.google.earth.kml`, so
     without this the .kml half had exactly the fault the .gpx half had — an identifier
     bound to nothing. Importing is how an app says "I handle someone else's type"; if
     another installed app does export it, the system merges rather than conflicts. -->
<key>UTImportedTypeDeclarations</key>
<array>
	<dict>
		<key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
		<string>com.google.earth.kml</string>
		<key>UTTypeDescription</key>
		<string>Keyhole Markup Language</string>
		<key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
		<array>
			<string>public.xml</string>
		</array>
		<key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
		<dict>
			<key>public.filename-extension</key>
			<array>
				<string>kml</string>
			</array>
			<key>public.mime-type</key>
			<array>
				<string>application/vnd.google-earth.kml+xml</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</dict>
</array>

<key>CFBundleDocumentTypes</key>
<array>
	<dict>
		<key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
		<string>Route</string>
		<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
		<string>Viewer</string>
		<key>LSHandlerRank</key>
		<string>Alternate</string>
		<key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
		<array>
			<string>cc.meteoride.gpx</string>
			<string>com.google.earth.kml</string>
		</array>
	</dict>
</array>

<!-- Copy opened documents into the app sandbox instead of editing them in place. -->
<key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
<false/>

<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
<string>MeteoRide uses your location only to centre the map on where you are when you open the app with no route loaded. For example, if you open it while travelling, the map starts on the town you are in instead of a default city. Your position is not saved or sent to the weather services: forecasts use the points of your route. You can decline and still use the whole app.</string>

<!-- Rotating breaks the layout badly, so the app is locked to portrait. Capacitor's
     template ships all four orientations for both idioms; set both keys rather than
     leave the iPad one to fall back, since `cap add ios` targets iPhone and iPad alike
     unless that is changed by hand. -->
<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
<array>
	<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
</array>
<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
<array>
	<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
	<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
	<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
</array>

<!-- Ride alerts: the background task the runner schedules. The identifier must equal
     plugins.BackgroundRunner.label in mobile/capacitor.config.json. Xcode adds
     UIBackgroundModes itself when "Background fetch" is
     ticked under Signing & Capabilities → Background Modes; listed here so a hand-edited
     plist ends up the same. -->
<key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
<array>
	<string>cc.meteoride.app.watch</string>
</array>
<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
<array>
	<string>fetch</string>
</array>

<!-- Location purpose strings. The app only ever asks for "when in use" — `centreOnUser`
     reads the position once, at launch, to centre the map — but Apple's upload check is
     static: it scans for *references* to the always-authorization APIs, and two installed
     plugins carry them. @capacitor/geolocation names
     NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription in its constants, and
     @capacitor/background-runner names the older NSLocationAlwaysUsageDescription. Without
     both, the upload comes back as ITMS-90683 ("Missing purpose string"), which is a
     warning on delivery and a rejection at review.

     Declaring the strings does not make the app request always-authorization; nothing in
     this code calls for it. The wording says so, because these are shown to the user if a
     dependency ever does ask, and a string that promised something else would be a lie
     told on the app's behalf. Keep all three in step with what public/privacy-ios.html
     says about location. -->
<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
<string>MeteoRide uses your location only to centre the map on where you are when you open the app with no route loaded. For example, if you open it while travelling, the map starts on the town you are in instead of a default city. Your position is not saved or sent to the weather services: forecasts use the points of your route. You can decline and still use the whole app.</string>
<key>NSLocationAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
<string>MeteoRide uses your location only to centre the map on where you are when you open the app with no route loaded. For example, if you open it while travelling, the map starts on the town you are in instead of a default city. Your position is not saved or sent to the weather services: forecasts use the points of your route. You can decline and still use the whole app.</string>

<!-- Export compliance. MeteoRide implements no cryptography of its own and ships none:
     no algorithms, no key material, no encrypted storage. What it uses is what the
     system provides — TLS for every request, and whatever hashing or randomness the
     platform and its dependencies reach for (Capacitor calls CommonCrypto's SHA-256;
     the background runner exposes system randomness). Those are system services, not an
     implementation of this app's, which is the exemption Apple describes. The route
     fingerprints and toGeoJSON's hashes are non-cryptographic. So the answer is always
     the same one —
     and declaring it here means App Store Connect stops asking on every single upload
     instead of once. Removing this key does not change what the app does; it only puts
     the question back. If MeteoRide ever ships its own crypto, this has to go and the
     real questionnaire has to be answered.
     https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/complying-with-encryption-export-regulations -->
<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
<false/>
